Box-Sizing is a CSS property which defines how the total height and width of a container is calculated.
Its properties are used to include or exclude the border-width and padding into the total width or height of the element.

The two common values of Box-sizing are,
i) border-box : It implies that the width or height set on an element are distributed among the content, border and padding dimensions.
The final dimensions of the element remains the same as set upon it because it is adjusted between the content, padding and border.
Final dimensions = Set Dimensions 

ii) content-box : It implies that the width or height set on an element is applied completely on the content of the element. 
The dimensions of border and padding are added extra to find the final dimensions of the element. 
Final dimensions = Set Dimension + Padding + Border. 

Border-Box is generally more preferred over content-box for box-sizing because it provides more responsive and predictable 
layout as compared to the content-box.
